| Topic                        | Explanation                                                                                                                                                                      | Example                                 |
| ---------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| **What is Python?**          | Python is a high-level, general-purpose programming language known for readable syntax and a large standard library.                                                             | `print("Hello World")`                  |
| **Who created Python?**      | Python was created by **Guido van Rossum**.                                                                                                                                      | —                                       |
| **First released**           | Python was first released publicly in **1991**.                                                                                                                                  | —                                       |
| **Python's name**            | The name "Python" was inspired by the comedy group/show **Monty Python**, rather than the snake.                                                                                 | —                                       |
| **High-level language**      | Python hides many low-level machine details, making programming easier for humans.                                                                                               | `x = 10`                                |
| **General-purpose language** | Python can be used for many different types of applications.                                                                                                                     | Web, automation, AI, data science       |
| **Interpreted nature**       | Python is commonly described as interpreted because Python programs are executed by the Python runtime rather than compiled directly into native machine code by the programmer. | `python app.py`                         |
| **Dynamically typed**        | You don't normally declare a variable's type explicitly. Python determines the type at runtime.                                                                                  | `x = 10`                                |
| **Object-oriented**          | Python supports classes, objects, inheritance, encapsulation, and polymorphism.                                                                                                  | `class Student:`                        |
| **Procedural programming**   | Python can also be written as a sequence of procedures/functions.                                                                                                                | `def add():`                            |
| **Functional programming**   | Python supports functional programming features such as functions as objects, `lambda`, `map()`, and `filter()`.                                                                 | `square = lambda x: x*x`                |
| **Open source**              | Python is open-source and freely available.                                                                                                                                      | —                                       |
| **Cross-platform**           | Python programs can run on Windows, Linux, macOS, and other supported platforms.                                                                                                 | `python program.py`                     |
| **Readable syntax**          | Python syntax is designed to be relatively easy to read and write.                                                                                                               | `if age >= 18:`                         |
| **Indentation**              | Python uses indentation to define blocks of code.                                                                                                                                | `if x > 5:`<br>    `print(x)`           |
| **Case-sensitive**           | Python treats uppercase and lowercase names as different.                                                                                                                        | `name` ≠ `Name`                         |
| **Comments**                 | Comments explain code and are ignored during normal execution.                                                                                                                   | `# This is a comment`                   |
| **Variables**                | Variables are names that refer to values/objects.                                                                                                                                | `age = 20`                              |
| **Data Types**               | Python provides built-in types such as numbers, strings, lists, tuples, sets, dictionaries, and Booleans.                                                                        | `x = 10`                                |
| **Integer**                  | Represents whole numbers.                                                                                                                                                        | `age = 25`                              |
| **Float**                    | Represents decimal numbers.                                                                                                                                                      | `price = 99.50`                         |
| **String**                   | Represents text.                                                                                                                                                                 | `name = "John"`                         |
| **Boolean**                  | Represents truth values: `True` or `False`.                                                                                                                                      | `is_valid = True`                       |
| **None**                     | Represents the absence of a value.                                                                                                                                               | `result = None`                         |
| **List**                     | Ordered, mutable collection.                                                                                                                                                     | `numbers = [1, 2, 3]`                   |
| **Tuple**                    | Ordered, immutable collection.                                                                                                                                                   | `point = (10, 20)`                      |
| **Set**                      | Collection of unique elements.                                                                                                                                                   | `nums = {1, 2, 3}`                      |
| **Dictionary**               | Collection of key-value pairs.                                                                                                                                                   | `student = {"name": "John", "age": 20}` |
| **Input**                    | `input()` gets data from the user and returns it as a string.                                                                                                                    | `name = input("Name: ")`                |
| **Output**                   | `print()` displays information.                                                                                                                                                  | `print("Hello")`                        |
| **Type conversion**          | Converts a value from one type to another when possible.                                                                                                                         | `int("10")`                             |
| **Operators**                | Operators perform calculations, comparisons, assignments, logical operations, etc.                                                                                               | `a + b`                                 |
| **Arithmetic**               | Used for mathematical calculations.                                                                                                                                              | `+ - * / // % **`                       |
| **Comparison**               | Compares values and produces a Boolean result.                                                                                                                                   | `x > 10`                                |
| **Logical**                  | Combines or reverses conditions.                                                                                                                                                 | `and`, `or`, `not`                      |
| **Assignment**               | Assigns or updates values.                                                                                                                                                       | `x = 10`, `x += 2`                      |
| **Membership**               | Checks whether a value exists in a collection.                                                                                                                                   | `"a" in "apple"`                        |
| **Identity**                 | Checks whether references refer to the same object.                                                                                                                              | `x is None`                             |
| **Conditional Statements**   | Used to make decisions.                                                                                                                                                          | `if`, `elif`, `else`                    |
| **`if`**                     | Executes code when a condition is true.                                                                                                                                          | `if age >= 18:`                         |
| **`elif`**                   | Checks another condition if previous conditions were false.                                                                                                                      | `elif age >= 13:`                       |
| **`else`**                   | Executes when previous conditions are false.                                                                                                                                     | `else:`                                 |
| **Loops**                    | Repeat code.                                                                                                                                                                     | `for`, `while`                          |
| **`for` Loop**               | Iterates over an iterable.                                                                                                                                                       | `for x in numbers:`                     |
| **`while` Loop**             | Repeats while a condition is true.                                                                                                                                               | `while x < 10:`                         |
| **`break`**                  | Stops the nearest loop.                                                                                                                                                          | `if x == 5: break`                      |
| **`continue`**               | Skips the current loop iteration.                                                                                                                                                | `if x == 5: continue`                   |
| **`pass`**                   | Does nothing; commonly used as a placeholder.                                                                                                                                    | `if x > 5: pass`                        |
| **Functions**                | Reusable blocks of code.                                                                                                                                                         | `def add(a, b):`                        |
| **Parameters**               | Variables defined in a function declaration.                                                                                                                                     | `def add(a, b):`                        |
| **Arguments**                | Values passed to a function.                                                                                                                                                     | `add(10, 20)`                           |
| **`return`**                 | Sends a value back from a function.                                                                                                                                              | `return a + b`                          |
| **Modules**                  | Python files containing reusable code.                                                                                                                                           | `import math`                           |
| **Packages**                 | Collections of modules organized into packages.                                                                                                                                  | `import package.module`                 |
| **Libraries**                | Collections of reusable functionality; Python has a large standard library and a huge ecosystem of third-party packages.                                                         | `math`, `requests`, `pandas`            |
| **Exception Handling**       | Handles runtime errors without necessarily stopping the whole program unexpectedly.                                                                                              | `try`, `except`                         |
| **`try`**                    | Contains code that might raise an exception.                                                                                                                                     | `try: x = 10 / 0`                       |
| **`except`**                 | Handles an exception.                                                                                                                                                            | `except ZeroDivisionError:`             |
| **`finally`**                | Runs after `try`/`except` processing, whether or not an exception occurred.                                                                                                      | `finally:`                              |
| **Classes**                  | Blueprints for creating objects.                                                                                                                                                 | `class Student:`                        |
| **Objects**                  | Instances created from classes.                                                                                                                                                  | `student = Student()`                   |
| **Inheritance**              | Allows a class to inherit behavior from another class.                                                                                                                           | `class Dog(Animal):`                    |
| **File Handling**            | Python can read and write files.                                                                                                                                                 | `open("data.txt")`                      |
| **Web Development**          | Python is used for web applications and APIs.                                                                                                                                    | Django, Flask, FastAPI                  |
| **Data Science**             | Python is widely used for data analysis and scientific computing.                                                                                                                | NumPy, pandas                           |
| **Machine Learning**         | Python is widely used for machine learning and AI.                                                                                                                               | scikit-learn, PyTorch                   |
| **Automation**               | Python can automate repetitive computer tasks.                                                                                                                                   | File processing, scripts                |
| **Web Scraping**             | Python can retrieve and process web data where permitted.                                                                                                                        | Beautiful Soup                          |
| **Game Development**         | Python can be used to create simple games and game prototypes.                                                                                                                   | Pygame                                  |
| **GUI Applications**         | Python can create desktop graphical applications.                                                                                                                                | Tkinter, PySide                         |
| **Database Programming**     | Python can connect to and work with databases.                                                                                                                                   | SQLite, PostgreSQL                      |
| **Testing**                  | Python has tools for automated software testing.                                                                                                                                 | `unittest`, `pytest`                    |
| **AI Development**           | Python is widely used for AI and machine-learning applications.                                                                                                                  | PyTorch, TensorFlow                     |
| **Python Interpreter**       | The Python interpreter/runtime reads and executes Python code.                                                                                                                   | `python app.py`                         |
| **REPL**                     | Interactive Python shell where you can enter Python code and immediately see results.                                                                                            | `>>> 2 + 3`                             |
| **`.py` File**               | Standard extension for Python source files.                                                                                                                                      | `main.py`                               |
| **`__name__`**               | A special variable that identifies the current module's execution context.                                                                                                       | `if __name__ == "__main__":`            |
| **Main Block**               | Common pattern for code that should run when a file is executed directly.                                                                                                        | `if __name__ == "__main__":`            |
| **PEP 8**                    | Python's widely used style guide for writing readable Python code.                                                                                                               | `student_name = "John"`                 |
| **Virtual Environment**      | Creates an isolated environment for a project's Python packages.                                                                                                                 | `python -m venv .venv`                  |
| **Package Manager**          | `pip` is commonly used to install Python packages.                                                                                                                               | `pip install requests`                  |
| **Standard Library**         | Python comes with many modules that provide commonly needed functionality.                                                                                                       | `import math`                           |
| **Third-party Package**      | A package developed outside the Python standard library.                                                                                                                         | `pip install pandas`                    |
| **Advantages**               | Readability, large ecosystem, productivity, cross-platform support, and broad application areas.                                                                                 | —                                       |
| **Limitations**              | It can use more memory and may be slower than some compiled languages for certain CPU-intensive workloads.                                                                       | —                                       |
| **Best for Beginners**       | Python's readable syntax makes it a popular first programming language.                                                                                                          | `print("Hello")`                        |

